hi there steve kaufman here again and  today i want to talk about  what i would call  false debates  in the realm of language learning or  language acquisition  what i mean by false debates i would say  that in my now 10 years or so that i've  been involved with the whole online  language learning community it seems to  me that there are two major sort of  points of contention  one is whether we should speak early  or whether we should speak  you know later in other words whether we  should focus on output or whether we  should focus on input so that's one  debate and the other debate is whether  we need grammar  or don't need grammar in order to learn  a language okay why i  consider these in a way false debates is  because  in both cases it's not a black and white  situation all right input versus output  of course at some point you have to  start outputting people learn languages  in order to output they want to speak  they want to communicate so  those people who focus on input  in most cases it's not because they  don't want to output it's because they  feel more comfortable  building up competence in the language  vocabulary comprehension before they  start  speaking and that is a personal decision  there could be some who simply want to  learn the language in order to read  books or under you know understand  movies so that they would be totally  passive but that's  not likely to be the case i think  there's very few people like it maybe  researchers or something like that most  people and and most people who do put a  lot of effort into input and comprehend  the language spoken and and when they  read they develop the ability to speak  however  people who want to speak early who have  an urge to speak who want to communicate  even poorly with whatever words they  have and they're struggling to  understand there's absolutely nothing  wrong with that and those people  necessarily have to invest time in input  activities even while they're speaking  they're listening they're gaining  something through input so  fundamentally there's no contradiction  with those two positions it's simply a  matter of what you prefer to do so to  that extent it's not a real debate just  do what you want to do some people say  yes but people wait too long and they  become afraid to speak i would think  that a person who's afraid to speak when  they have very little understanding of  the language  could in fact become less afraid if they  build up more confidence in their  ability in the language so it really  again comes down to personal preference  making sure you spend time with the  language now the other big debate debate  is the grammar versus  input now  i have said before and in my previous  video i said there are things that i  simply don't notice in  a new language and that it helps  sometimes to have this pointed out say  in a grammar explanation however  it's only after i've experienced this  several times that i even understand  this explanation and if i'm confronted  with the task of trying to remember when  i use this tense as opposed to that  tense  it's intimidating and if if the task of  learning a language  is presented to me as having to remember  this rule and the exceptions i find that  puts me off now there are people who  love that typically people who have a  lot of experience with languages and  they feel they can kind of shortcut and  get to the essence aha this language is  subject verb object and this other  language is the subject object verb or  something  i have personally never found that  useful when i went to learn japanese it  became immediately obvious to me that  the verb comes at the end so i didn't  need to see a theoretical explanation  that that is the word order but i can  see that it's possible some people find  that confusing so a quick reference to  an explanation like that can be helpful  and there are sometimes things that we  simply don't notice without an  explanation so again a mixture of some  degree of involvement with grammar to  the degree that we want to be involved  with grammar again a matter of personal  preference  and then  exposing yourself to as much of the com  content as possible finding things of  interest  beginning perhaps with simpler material  like our mini stories and ramping up to  where you can expose yourself to a lot  of audio and reading in the language  regularly referring back to  grammar issues when you're when you have  a problem and i find i do this i'll look  up the declension of a russian noun or  look up the conjugation of a of a  spanish verb when i'm interested and  then it sort of solves a problem that  i'm experiencing in the text  typically again if i'm trying to tag  this phrase for something now  bear in mind that i can't spend all of  my reading time tagging and doing all of  this sort of  careful  mining of the text i also want to get  that general exposure so you kind of  combine some degree of worrying about  the details in the nuts and bolts and  then a lot of just  getting the language in you and i have  referred to this 80 20.  you know the the the big picture should  be about 80  and the the picking at the nuts and  bolts 20 it's also sometimes referred to  as  you know  top to bottom which is the big picture  or bottom up where you're starting with  the bits and pieces and working your way  up to the the bigger picture but in in  any case here again it's not a either  this or either that i think it's a bit  of a mixture of both i think because the  grammar and you know nailing down the  basics which i don't think we can do  that this has been the dominant and  remains  to this day the dominant method of  language instruction it's good to point  out as krashen does repeatedly the  tremendous power of meaningful  compelling input and that in the end the  languages that we speak the best will be  those where we've had the greatest  amount of input and also where we have  used them but in order to be able to use  them a lot to have meaningful  conversations we need vocabulary we need  input so  to summarize then i think to some extent  those debates is more a question of  emphasis and that emphasis will depend  on our personal preference but one  should be open-minded to the fact that  different people react differently based  on these preferences  uh but  that uh  let's call it the revolutionary  value of krashen's emphasis on input has  been to move the sort of  dialogue in language instruction further  towards in favor of input and i think  that has been a very valuable  contribution one that  certainly reflects my views and  these were my views long before i  discovered stephen krashen these were my  views as a language learner so there you  have it a little bit about what i would  call the false debates in language  learning bye for now  and if you like my videos please  subscribe  [Music]  you
 

